# arvo-bitmask, backlog

> **Type-surface reminder**: new entries use `arvo::USize` /
> `Bool` / `UFixed<N, 0, S>` instead of `usize` / `bool` / `u*`;
> `notko::Maybe` / `Outcome` instead of `Option` / `Result`. See
> `rules/type-surface.md`.

## Wider-than-256 mask widths

Mask4096 / Mask1024 / Mask128 ergonomic instantiations are deferred
post-1.0 if a consumer surfaces the need. The chassis is already
generic over W; arvo's BitsContainerFor projection
currently caps logical widths at 256 via WideBits
plumbing. Mask widths beyond 256 are deferred post-1.0
until a consumer surfaces the need.


## MaskOps Width-typed const-generic on consumers

When a consumer binds generically via the abstract trait, the
call-site spelling takes a Width const value rather than a u16
literal. Document the Width construction pattern (`Width::from_u16`)
in cookbook once a real consumer surfaces.

## Concrete MaskOps W specialisations

This ships only the blanket `impl<W: [const] BitPrim>
const MaskOps<...> for Mask<W>`. Concrete per-W overrides (e.g.,
Mask\<QWord\<Hot\>\>, Mask\<Bits\<256, Hot, Unsigned\>\>) land
behind `min_specialization` when bench evidence shows a concrete W
beats the blanket. Likely candidates: SIMD shortcuts on QWord-wide
or 256-bit-wide masks where a hand-written intrinsic chain
outperforms the generic body. Tracked alongside #320 SIMD platform
expansion.

## BitMatrix iter contract in arvo-bits-contracts

A BitMatrix-shaped contract in arvo-bits-contracts (paralleling
BitSequence for flat bitmasks) would let row-access algorithms live
in the contract layer and be reused without depending on the
concrete arvo-bitmask chassis. Not needed by current consumers
(arvo-graph uses BitMatrix<W, N> directly); add when a second
consumer shape surfaces.
